date 2 orders 1.1 × and candidate 7 orders 1.2 ×. The average multiplier is 1.046, so these are about −4.6 %, +5.4 % and +15 % against average demand.\n- **Product 0.** Going from 1.0 to 1.1 adds 109 units, costing about 763. It recovers about 46 lost sales worth about 24 each, around 1100, and adds about 320 storage. Net is close to zero, so this is a tie.\n- **Product 1.** Going from 1.0 to 1.1 adds 57 units, costing about 456. It recovers about 24 lost sales worth about 23 each, around 550, and adds about 210 storage. Net is about −110, so candidate 6 is better.\n- **Candidate 7 (1.2).** Clearly worse: each extra 10 % costs about 770 plus storage but recovers only about 400.\n\n**The 220 limit hurts candidate 2.** On day 22 both products arrive together: 89 of product 0 and 79 of product 1. Candidate 2 has built up about 60 extra units of product 0 and about 30 of product 1 by then. That totals about 257, so roughly 35–40 units of product 1 are lost. That happens just before the day-22 demand spike (forecast 52), which causes more shortages. Candidate 6 has about 175 on the same day and loses nothing.\n\n**Other candidates.**\n- **Candidate 3** is the same as candidate 2 but orders daily, so it is about 240 worse because of the fees.\n- **Candidate 1** is candidate 6's quantities ordered daily, so it is also about 240 worse.\n- **Candidate 4** pays the daily fees and ignores both spikes: day 17 for product 0 (100) and day 22 for product 1 (52).\n- **Candidate 0** pays the daily fees and orders 1.25 × forecast, which builds up stock it can't sell.\n\nOverall, candidate 6 is at least as good as candidate 2 on product 0, better on product 1, and has no losses from going over 220."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 354, "cache_read_input_tokens": 6882, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 354}, "output_tokens": 8